Erasmus:
Bridge
Score:0
Moves:0
The gale tears at you, biting deep within, and you know that if you don't make 
landfall soon you'll all be dead. You are John Blackthorne, Pilot-Major of a 
dead fleet: one ship left out of five, eight and twenty men out of one hundred 
and seven, and only ten of those can walk. Little food, almost no water, and 
that brackish and foul.

Bridge
This is the bridge of the Erasmus, a Dutch merchant and privateer. The unlashed
wheel is directly forward of you, a sea chair is lashed to the deck aft of the 
wheel, and the ship's bell is hanging here. Spray blows past in an angry 
torrent.

The wheel dominates the quarterdeck. It is turned straight and free to turn 
now.

The ship heels in a sudden squall, throwing you from your post at the wheel, 
which, uncontrolled, begins to turn to port.

>straighten the wheel
You take the wheel and straighten it. Helped by the following wind, the ship 
returns to its course.

Hendrik Specz, the third mate, appears on deck, pulling himself painfully up 
the gangway. He joins you on the bridge. "Go below, Pilot. I take this watch if
it pleases you." Gray with fatigue, he leans heavily against the binnacle and 
retches. "Blessed Lord Jesus help me!"

Bridge; course west; wheel straight
5
1
>lash the wheel
You loop the protective lashing around the spokes of the wheel. It is now 
locked in place.

"Where's the landfall you promised us? Where's the Japans? Where is it, I ask?"

Bridge2
>specz, take the wheel
"Aye, Pilot, I take the wheel."

"Perhaps there is no Japans," Hendrik mutters scornfully. "It's just a 
Gottbewonden legend."

Bridge3
>down
Deck
This is the main deck of the Erasmus. A companionway leads up to the bridge, 
and another one forward leads below decks to the crew quarters. A stairway aft 
leads to the officers' cabins.

The anchor hangs over the side, ready to be dropped if ever, by the grace of 
God and your good piloting, the Erasmus reaches port.

You are tired, deathly tired, and near starving. With a start, you realize it's
time to go below and send out replacements for the men on watch, by now 
freezing and windburned. It's past time to eat some of your meager cache of 
food; a few shrivelled apples. Time, too, for the unpleasant task of visiting 
Captain Spillbergen, still in nominal command but deathly sick, confined to his
cabin. It's also time to write in your rutter, though God knows there is 
nothing new since yesterday; nothing, that is, but death.

