It is near noon and you are standing on the quarterdeck of the galley under an 
overcast sky. During the first night the galley had sped southward down the 
east coast of the Izu peninsula. When it came abreast of the southmost cape, 
Cape Ito, Rodrigues had set the course West South West and had left the safety 
of the coast for the open sea, heading for a landfall at Cape Shinto two 
hundred miles away.

"What do you think, Ingeles?" asks Rodrigues.
"I think there'll be a storm," you reply.
"When?"
"Before sunset" you answer.

The storm comes before sunset and catches you out to sea. Land is ten miles 
away.

Toranaga's Galley
You are standing on the quarterdeck of Toranaga's galley, with a good view 
forward to the oar deck.

A wooden life ring hangs nearby.

Rodrigues stands at the helm.

The helm is here, turned to port now. The life line secures you as the deck 
pitches and rolls.

"Watch out forward!" Rodrigues shouts. The galley rolls sickeningly, twenty 
oars pulling at air instead of sea and there is chaos aboard. The first comber 
has struck and the port gunwale is awash. The galley is floundering.

